        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Start with the four tools you will touch on every fly, then add specialty pieces when a pattern asks for them.</p><h3>The first four</h3><p>Vise, one sharp fine scissors, a bobbin, and a whip finisher. That set ties most trout flies without drama.</p><h3>Scissors</h3><p>Fine tips for thread and detail. Keep a tougher pair for wire, foam, and coarse stuff so the fine tips stay sharp.</p><h3>Bobbins</h3><p>Ceramic or smooth tubes that do not fray thread. Weight and balance are personal. Buy what you will hold for an hour without thinking about it.</p><h3>Specialty</h3><p>Hackle pliers, hair stackers, dubbing brushes, and caddies earn a drawer when you repeat the same pattern enough times to notice the slowdown.</p><h3>Vises</h3><p>The vise lives next door under <a href=\"/collections/fly-tying-vises\">Fly Tying Vises</a>. Materials for the next bag sit under <a href=\"/collections/fly-tying-materials\">Fly Tying Materials</a>.</p>"
