      "blocks": {
        "q4": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How should I care for fly tying tools?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Keep edges clean and dry. Wipe wax and adhesive off bobbin tubes, bodkins, and vise jaws before it builds up. Oil a sticky vise joint lightly.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Do I need a full tool kit?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Kits are fine if the scissors and bobbin are actually usable. Otherwise buy the four core tools separately and skip filler you will not touch.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Do I need a whip finisher as a beginner?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Yes, sooner than most people think. Half-hitches work. A whip finish is cleaner, smaller, and less likely to unravel when you varnish. Start with the Hareline Standard.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Standard vs extended whip finisher?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Standard for trout dries, nymphs, and most soft hackles. Extended when the head sits farther from the vise jaws on big streamers and Intruders.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Tool kit or buy pieces?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>A Loon Core or Complete kit is faster if your drawer is empty. Buy singles when you already own scissors and a bobbin and only need the whip finisher or pliers.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
